 routing or graph baselines remain competitive in AA, they still show positive forgetting after sequential\nL545@P6: adaptation. In contrast, MasFACT attains negative average AF, suggesting that transferred topology\nL546@P6: priors can improve later evaluation on previous tasks rather than merely slow degradation.\nL547@P6: Table 3 evaluates a more demanding setting where MMLU-Pro, MATH, and TACO are decomposed\nL548@P6: into 13, 7, and 8 semantically close increments. MasFACT is the only method that consistently\nL549@P6: obtains negative AF, showing that its topology priors not only mitigate forgetting but can also induce\nL550@P6: positive backward transfer when later tasks refine useful collaboration patterns. The table also exposes\nL551@P6: a MAS-specific failure mode: replay is not universally beneficial for topology generators. It can also\nL552@P6: reduce AA or increase forgetting, as seen for GDesigner on MMLU-Pro and ARGDesigner on MATH,\nL553@P6-7: 7Table 4: Plug-and-play generalization of MasFACT across MAS frameworks. Green arrows report\nL554@P7: absolute gains for AA and absolute reductions for AF.\nL555@P7: Backbone Variant\nL556@P7: Class-level CL Domain-level CL Task-level CL\nL557@P7: MMLU-Pro MATH Code Multi-hop QA KQA→Math\nL558@P7: →Code→MQA\nL559@P7: AA↑ AF↓ AA↑ AF↓ AA↑ AF↓ AA↑ AF↓ AA↑ AF↓\nL560@P7: AGP Original 43.49 4.37 50.80 4.03 51.89 3.92 43.05 4.29 37.22 6.70\nL561@P7: + MasFACT 48.39 ↑4.90 1.49 ↓2.88 54.36 ↑3.56 0.90 ↓3.13 57.21 ↑5.32 2.06 ↓1.86 49.13 ↑6.08 1.57 ↓2.72 40.21 ↑2.99 3.04 ↓3.66\nL562@P7: GDesigner Original 45.04 3.28 51.92 3.89 54.46 4.89 44.92 5.23 41.81 6.95\nL563@P7: + MasFACT 47.93 ↑2.89 0.79 ↓2.49 55.98 ↑4.06 0.68 ↓3.21 59.14 ↑4.68 1.43 ↓3.46 48.32 ↑3.40 0.23 ↓5.00 46.53 ↑4.72 2.49 ↓4.46\nL564@P7: ARGDesigner Original 44.72 3.25 53.84 3.62 52.47 4.09 46.32 4.27 42.18 6.14\nL565@P7: + MasFACT 48.45 ↑3.73 1.21 ↓2.04 55.93 ↑2.09 0.52 ↓3.10 58.76 ↑6.29 1.05 ↓3.04 50.53 ↑4.21 0.31 ↓3.96 48.44 ↑6.26 3.32 ↓2.82\nL566@P7: indicating that forgetting arises not only from sample distribution shift, but also from drift in the\nL567@P7: structure-generation policy.\nL568@P7: Replay without topology constraints may mix incompatible collaboration modes and blur fine-grained\nL569@P7: specialization. This effect becomes more evident in highly similar task increments. Thus, task\nL570@P7: similarity does not necessarily imply topology compatibility. Over-replay can dilute current topology\nL571@P7: and harm specialization. MasFACT avoids this by factorizing historical structures into consensus\nL572@P7: priors and task-specific residuals, then applying FGW-constrained residual adaptation.\nL573@P7: Plug-and-Play Continual Topology Transfer. To evaluate whether MasFACT is tied to a specific\nL574@P7: topology generator, we attach its continual topology layer to three representative recent and widely\nL575@P7: adopted paradigms of adaptive MAS optimizers, AGP [21], GDesigner [45], and ARGDesigner [24],\nL576@P7: covering pruning-based, graph-generation-based, and autoregressive graph-design paradigms. Im\u0002plementation details are provided in Appendix G.2. As shown in Table 4, MasFACT consistently\nL577@P7: enhances performance across all backbones in class-, domain-, and task-incremental continual learning\nL578@P7: settings. This supports its role as an architecture-agnostic continual topology layer rather than a\nL579@P7: generator-specific trick. The gains indicate that structural priors provide not only regularization,\nL580@P7: but also better topology initialization and a more stable search direction, especially for dynamic\nL581@P7: optimizers that otherwise adapt aggressively and drift from previously useful collaboration patterns.\nL582@P7: Table 5: Ablation study on the core designs under\nL583@P7: MATH 10-shot class-incremental setting.\nL584@P7: Method AA↑ AF↓ ∆AA ∆AF\nL585@P7: MasFACT 59.10 -0.41 – –\nL586@P7: w/o Historical Prior 54.63 2.36 -4.47 +2.77\nL587@P7: w/o FGW Alignment 55.13 0.76 -3.97 +1.17\nL588@P7: Euclidean Alignment 54.46 0.94 -4.64 +1.35\nL589@P7: GW-only Alignment 56.21 0.38 -2.89 +0.79\nL590@P7: w/o Residual Edit 55.01 0.03 -4.09 +0.44\nL591@P7: w/o PAC-Bayes Constraint 54.71 1.27 -4.39 +1.68\nL592@P7: L2 Complexity 56.90 0.61 -2.20 +1.02\nL593@P7: Ablation Study. Table 5 examines the individual\nL594@P7: contribution of each core design in MasFACT.\nL595@P7: (1) Historical prior. Removing the historical\nL596@P7: topology prior causes the largest degradation,\nL597@P7: showing that continual MAS topology learn\u0002ing cannot be solved by current-task adaptation\nL598@P7: alone; reusable collaboration structures must be\nL599@P7: explicitly retained. (2) Geometry-aware align\u0002ment. The alignment variants confirm that cross\u0002task transfer requires jointly matching both topol\u0002ogy geometry and agent semantics. Euclidean\nL600@P7: alignment performs worse than the no-FGW vari\u0002ant, suggesting that index-wise correspondence\nL601@P7: can misalign agents under role or node-order\nL602@P7: drift. GW-only alignment remains inferior to full FGW, because relational structure alone cannot\nL603@P7: disambiguate agent semantics. Full FGW provides a more reliable transfer map by jointly aligning\nL604@P7: agent attributes and communication relations. (3) Conservative posterior adaptation. The residual\nL605@P7: and complexity-control ablations reveal MasFACT’s stability–plasticity mechanism. Removing\nL606@P7: residual editing weakens task-specific adaptation, whereas removing the PAC-Bayes constraint or\nL607@P7: replacing it with an L2 penalty increases forgetting. This confirms that MasFACT’s gains come from\nL608@P7: prior-centered posterior control rather than generic regularization.\nL609@P7: Mechanistic Analysis of Structural Forgetting. We examine whether continual MAS degradation\nL610@P7: is coupled with drift in the learned communication topology. As shown in Figure 3(a), the isolated\nL611@P7: topology optimizer suffers a sharp old-task accuracy drop as topology drift increases, indicating\nL612@P7: that new-task adaptation moves the generator away from previously effective collaboration patterns.\nL613@P7: Replay reduces but does not eliminate this drift, while removing the PAC-Bayes constraint still allows\nL614@P7: posterior updates to accumulate structural deviation despite aligned priors. In contrast, MasFACT\nL615@P7-8: 81 2 3 4 5 6 7\nL616@P8: Task stage\nL617@P8: 75\nL618@P8: 70\nL619@P8: 65\nL620@P8: 60\nL621@P8: 55\nL622@P8: Average accuracy 50\nL623@P8: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\nL624@P8: Task stage\nL625@P8: 0.0\nL626@P8: 0.2\nL627@P8: 0.4\nL628@P8: 0.6\nL629@P8: Topology drift\nL630@P8: Isolated Topology Optimizer\nL631@P8: w/o PAC-Bayes Constraint\nL632@P8: +Replay\nL633@P8: MasFACT\nL634@P8: (a) Topology forgetting dynamics.\nL635@P8: Close Moderate Distant\nL636@P8: −1\nL637@P8: 0\nL638@P8: 1\nL639@P8: 2\nL640@P8: 3\nL641@P8: 4\nL642@P8: Average Forgetting (%)\nL643@P8: -0.62\nL644@P8: 1.18\nL645@P8: 3.74 κt combines:\nL646@P8: alignment cost,\nL647@P8: prior dispersion,\nL648@P8: posterior deviation\nL649@P8: κt = z(At) + z(σt) + z(Rt)\nL650@P8: (b) Forgetting by prior-transfer distance.\nL651@P8: Figure 3: Mechanistic analysis of structural forgetting. Left: topology drift tracks old-task accuracy\nL652@P8: degradation. Right: forgetting increases as the retrieved prior becomes more distant.\nL653@P8: maintains the flattest accuracy trajectory and the lowest topology drift, supporting that continual MAS\nL654@P8: forgetting is a topology-level phenomenon rather than merely a sample-distribution issue.\nL655@P8: Figure 3(b) links this trend to the PAC-Bayes transfer analysis. We group stages into close, moderate,\nL656@P8: and distant prior-transfer regimes by κt, which combines FGW alignment cost, prior dispersion, and\nL657@P8: posterior deviation. Forgetting grows monotonically with prior-transfer distance: close transfers\nL658@P8: can yield backward transfer, whereas distant transfers cause substantial forgetting. The monotonic\nL659@P8: pattern supports our transfer-complexity term, showing that retention benefits only from well-aligned,\nL660@P8: stable priors rather than indiscriminate prior reuse. This explains why MasFACT must jointly control\nL661@P8: alignment mismatch, prior instability, and posterior drift. Sensitivity results in Appendix G.1 confirm\nL662@P8: that this trend is not due to hyperparameter tuning.\nL663@P8: 5 Related Work\nL664@P8: Multi-Agent System Topology Optimization. Recent LLM-based MAS improve reasoning and\nL665@P8: planning by organizing role-specialized agents [16] into structured collaborative workflows [41, 22].\nL666@P8: Prior studies mainly focus on conversation protocols [17, 49], task routing [44, 36], and workflow\nL667@P8: orchestration [29, 3] to coordinate inter-agent communication. Beyond manually designed templates,\nL668@P8: recent topology optimization approaches explicitly treat MAS communication structures as learnable\nL669@P8: objects [48], constructing task-adaptive collaboration graphs through graph search [7, 51], topology\nL670@P8: generation [47], autoregressive graph construction [24], or joint prompt–workflow optimization [52,\nL671@P8: 46]. Representative systems such as G-Designer and ARG-Designer formulate topology design as\nL672@P8: task-feedback-conditioned graph generation [45, 24]. However, these methods are primarily developed\nL673@P8: for static task distributions or one-shot optimization, leaving open how a topology generator can adapt\nL674@P8: from limited new-task data while preserving previously effective collaboration structures, especially\nL675@P8: under task-incremental shifts with large inter-task gaps. In contrast, our work studies continual MAS\nL676@P8: topology learning and explicitly addresses topology forgetting through transferable structural priors\nL677@P8: and conservative topology adaptation.\nL678@P8: Continual Learning for Agents. Continual learning typically mitigates catastrophic forgetting [19]\nL679@P8: through parameter regularization, replay, parameter isolation, or parameter-efficient adaptation [25,\nL680@P8: 27, 26]. These methods are mainly developed for single-model or fixed-architecture settings, where\nL681@P8: knowledge is encoded in parameters, latent representations [37], or stored samples [11]. In LLM-based\nL682@P8: MAS, however, task knowledge is also embodied in collaboration structures and communication\nL683@P8: relations among agents, so preserving model capabilities alone does not ensure preservation of\nL684@P8: effective coordination topologies. Although recent studies have explored continual adaptation for\nL685@P8: agent systems and LLMs [42], they largely focus on capability retention or memory representations,\nL686@P8: leaving topology-level forgetting under evolving agent interactions insufficiently studied. Our work\nL687@P8: instead treats historical collaboration topologies as transferable structural priors and formulates\nL688@P8: continual MAS adaptation as geometry-aware posterior transfer over evolving topology spaces.\nL689@P8-9: 96 Conclusion\nL690@P9: We studied continual topology learning for MAS, a setting that requires new-task adaptation while\nL691@P9: preserving and transferring effective collaboration structures across a task stream. We identified\nL692@P9: topology forgetting as a structural failure mode of continual MAS adaptation, and proposed MasFACT,\nL693@P9: a geometry-aware framework that turns historical high-utility topologies into transferable priors. By\nL694@P9: factorizing historical topology knowledge, aligning prior atoms through FGW optimal transport, and\nL695@P9: adapting stochastic topology posteriors under PAC-Bayes-guided complexity control, MasFACT\nL696@P9: enables new tasks to reuse validated collaboration skeletons while performing only conservative\nL697@P9: task-specific edits. We further introduce a hierarchical continual MAS evaluation protocol covering\nL698@P9: multi-granularity shifts across diverse reasoning families. Experiments across class-, domain-, and\nL699@P9: task-level continual settings show that MasFACT improves average accuracy, reduces forgetting, and\nL700@P9: can serve as a plug-and-play continual topology layer for different MAS optimizers.\nL701@P9: References\nL702@P9: [1] Jacob Austin, Augustus Odena, Maxwell Nye, Maarten Bosma, Henryk Michalewski, David\nL703@P9: Dohan, Ellen Jiang, Carrie Cai, Michael Terry, Quoc Le, et al. Program synthesis with large\nL704@P9: language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.07732, 2021.\nL705@P9: [2] Mark Chen, Jerry Tworek, Heewoo Jun, Qiming Yuan, Henrique Ponde de Oliveira Pinto, Jared\nL706@P9: Kaplan, Harri Edwards, Yuri Burda, Nicholas Joseph, Greg Brockman, et al. Evaluating large\nL707@P9: language models trained on code. arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.03374, 2021.\nL708@P9: [3] Weize Chen, Yusheng Su, Jingwei Zuo, Cheng Yang, Chenfei Yuan, Chi-Min Chan, Heyang Yu,\nL709@P9: Yaxi Lu, Yi-Hsin Hung, Chen Qian, et al. Agentverse: Facilitating multi-agent collaboration\nL710@P9: and exploring emergent behaviors. In The Twelfth International Conference on Learning\nL711@P9: Representations, 2023.\nL712@P9: [4] Wenhu Chen, Ming Yin, Max Ku, et al. Theoremqa: A theorem-driven question answering\nL713@P9: dataset. In Proceedings of the 2023 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language\nL714@P9: Processing (EMNLP), 2023.\nL715@P9: [5] Karl Cobbe, Vineet Kosaraju, Mohammad Bavarian, Mark Chen, Heewoo Jun, Lukasz Kaiser,\nL716@P9: Matthias Plappert, Jerry Tworek, Jacob Hilton, Reiichiro Nakano, et al. Training verifiers to\nL717@P9: solve math word problems. arXiv preprint arXiv:2110.14168, 2021.\nL718@P9: [6] Yilun Du, Shuang Li, Antonio Torralba, Joshua B Tenenbaum, and Igor Mordatch. Improv\u0002ing factuality and reasoning in language models through multiagent debate. In Forty-first\nL719@P9: international co