erarchical continual MAS evaluation protocol\nL1707@P27: described in Appendix E. We evaluate continual adaptation across task-level, domain-level, and\nL1708@P27: class-level streams over four reasoning families: knowledge QA, mathematical reasoning, code\nL1709@P27: generation, and knowledge-grounded multi-hop reasoning. At each continual stage, the model is\nL1710@P27: adapted on the current support set and evaluated on all seen tasks. Old-task evaluation is performed\nL1711@P27: in read-only mode: no gradient updates, memory writing, or test-time adaptation are allowed.\nL1712@P27: Few-shot and continual settings. For few-shot continual learning, the base task uses the data-rich\nL1713@P27: base split specified by the protocol, while each subsequent task provides only a small support set.\nL1714@P27: For standard continual learning, each new stage uses the corresponding full training subset. For\nL1715@P27: replay-based variants, replay examples are drawn only from previously seen training data according\nL1716@P27: to the replay budget specified in the configuration. The exact number of examples per stage, replay\nL1717@P27: budget, and evaluation limit are provided in the released configuration files.\nL1718@P27: Metrics. We report average accuracy (AA) and average forgetting (AF) as the primary continual\u0002learning metrics. AA measures the average performance over all seen tasks after a continual stage,\nL1719@P27: while AF measures the average degradation of previous-task performance relative to its best historical\nL1720@P27: value. For task-specific evaluation, we use the standard metric associated with each benchmark\nL1721@P27: family, such as exact-match or multiple-choice accuracy for QA, answer-equivalence accuracy\nL1722@P27: for mathematics, executable test-based correctness for code generation, and exact-match/F1-style\nL1723@P27: evaluation for multi-hop QA when applicable.\nL1724@P27: Random seeds and statistical reporting. Each main experiment is repeated with five different\nL1725@P27: random seeds. Unless otherwise stated, all reported results are averaged over these five runs. We\nL1726@P27-28: 28Table 12: Ablation study on the core designs under MATH 10-shot class-incremental setting. Results\nL1727@P28: include standard deviations over five random seeds.\nL1728@P28: Method AA↑ AF↓ ∆AA ∆AF\nL1729@P28: MasFACT 59.10±0.42 -0.41±0.18 – –\nL1730@P28: w/o Historical Prior 54.63±0.61 2.36±0.27 -4.47 +2.77\nL1731@P28: w/o FGW Alignment 55.13±0.47 0.76±0.22 -3.97 +1.17\nL1732@P28: Euclidean Alignment 54.46±0.55 0.94±0.25 -4.64 +1.35\nL1733@P28: GW-only Alignment 56.21±0.91 0.38±0.36 -2.89 +0.79\nL1734@P28: w/o Residual Edit 55.01±0.39 0.03±0.26 -4.09 +0.44\nL1735@P28: w/o PAC-Bayes Constraint 54.71±0.29 1.27±0.14 -4.39 +1.68\nL1736@P28: L2 Complexity 56.90±0.46 0.61±0.21 -2.20 +1.02\nL1737@P28: report standard deviations for the main results and ablation studies; when space is limited in the main\nL1738@P28: text, complete mean–standard-deviation tables are provided in the appendix. The random seeds affect\nL1739@P28: support-set sampling, replay sampling, topology sampling, and stochastic posterior adaptation.\nL1740@P28: Hyperparameters. The topology generator, training schedule, and basic optimization settings\nL1741@P28: follow the underlying MAS backbone, GDesigner [45], to ensure a fair comparison with the base\nL1742@P28: system. We use the standard training procedure of GDesigner and only introduce the additional\nL1743@P28: hyperparameters required by MasFACT, including the FGW semantic–structural trade-off coefficient\nL1744@P28: ρ, the entropy coefficient ε, retrieval weights for alignment cost, prior dispersion and historical utility,\nL1745@P28: posterior-prior regularization weight, residual sparsity weight, and memory consolidation thresholds.\nL1746@P28: These hyperparameters are selected using validation performance on held-out stages and then fixed\nL1747@P28: across test streams within the same task family. Sensitivity analyses for key structural hyperparameters\nL1748@P28: are provided in Appendix G.1.\nL1749@P28: Compute resources. All experiments are conducted on a GPU cluster equipped with multi-core\nL1750@P28: Sugon x86 CPU nodes and four NVIDIA A100 GPUs per experiment. The dominant computational\nL1751@P28: cost comes from LLM-based MAS inference across multiple continual stages and five random seeds,\nL1752@P28: while the additional overhead of MasFACT’s topology memory, FGW alignment, and posterior\nL1753@P28: adaptation is comparatively small because each MAS graph contains a limited number of agents. The\nL1754@P28: released repository includes the configuration files needed to reproduce the reported runs under the\nL1755@P28: same evaluation protocol.\nL1756@P28: 29\n"
